Subject: Give the register VM's global reads a per-instruction cache

What this does
  `get_global` gains a cache site operand ("rab" becomes "rabs"), numbered with
  the function's property sites. When a read hits the runtime's global cell
  cache or fills it, the site records the global's property slot and the
  cell generation. The next execution compares the generation and loads the
  value through the slot: no atom hash, no set probe and no way compare.

  A site entry holds a property slot, not a shape, so the cache's mark and
  reset paths skip it. If the generation counter ever wraps, every function's
  global sites are cleared along with the context cells.

Why we need it
  On Octane mandreel `get_global` is 21% of all executed register
  instructions, at 22 machine instructions each through the 2-way cell cache,
  and 7 to 10% on richards and earley-boyer. Globals that share a cell set
  also evicted each other.

What it adds
  REG_PIC_GLOBAL            - the cache-entry kind of a global read site.
  regvm_global_site_fill()  - records a filled cell in an instruction's site.
  js_gv_site_flush()        - clears global sites when the generation wraps.
  regvm_t_get_global_cell() - the cell-cache half of the threaded read.

Needs a bytecode version bump
  Yes. `get_global`'s encoding gains an operand. The register builtin blobs
  are rebuilt in 9999.

